# Quectel container support (Docker / Podman) — M2 default kernel
# Tests on Quectel Pi M2 (RK3576, kernel 6.1.118-rt36) showed the stock
# rockchip_linux_defconfig leaves these off, which blocks standard
# `apt install docker.io` workflow:
#   * POSIX_MQUEUE -> runc: "mount mqueue to /dev/mqueue: no such device"
#   * BPF_SYSCALL + CGROUP_BPF -> runc: "bpf_prog_query(BPF_CGROUP_DEVICE)
#                            failed: function not implemented" (cgroup v1
#                            device controller uses eBPF)
#   * VETH         -> dockerd: "create veth pair: Operation not supported"
#                          (default bridge network needs a veth pair)
#   * BRIDGE_NETFILTER -> iptables nat over bridge; without it docker0
#                          cannot create the default NAT network
#   * NETFILTER_XT_MATCH_ADDRTYPE / _CONNTRACK -> the x_tables match modules
#                          that dockerd's default nat/filter rules resolve to
#                          (`-m addrtype`, `-m conntrack`). Only reachable
#                          once NFT_COMPAT is on.
#   * OVERLAY_FS       -> overlay2 storage driver; without it docker falls
#                          back to vfs/fuse-overlayfs (slow, extra package)
#   * MEMCG            -> docker stats / --memory / --memory-swap limits
#   * NFT_COMPAT   -> **KEYSTONE**. Debian's iptables 1.8.11 defaults to the
#                          nf_tables backend ("iptables v1.8.11 (nf_tables)").
#                          It translates only a small native set (limit/among/
#                          udp/tcp/mark) and wraps every other match and EVERY
#                          target into the nft expressions "match"/"target",
#                          which exist only in nft_compat. tristate with no
#                          default -> stock Rockchip defconfig leaves it off.
#                          Symptom: DOCKER chains get created (verdict-only
#                          rules need no compat) then dockerd dies on the first
#                          `-m ...` / `-j ...` rule:
#                            Warning: Extension addrtype revision 0 not
#                              supported, missing kernel module?   <- noise
#                            iptables: CHAIN_ADD failed (bad ...)  <- real
#   * NFT_NAT      -> the "nat" base-chain **type**. iptables-nft creates the
#                          nat table's built-in chains itself with
#                          .type = "nat"; that type lives in nft_chain_nat.o,
#                          guarded by NFT_NAT. "filter" is unaffected because
#                          nft_chain_filter.o is always part of nf_tables-objs.
#                          Symptom: nat table and user chains (-N DOCKER) do
#                          appear, but touching any nat base chain fails:
#                            iptables -t nat -I POSTROUTING ... -j MASQUERADE
#                            iptables: CHAIN_ADD failed (No such file or
#                              directory): chain POSTROUTING
#                          Only the chain TYPE is needed here; the native
#                          NFT_FIB_IPV4 / NFT_CT / NFT_MASQ expressions are
#                          still NOT required (dockerd's addrtype/conntrack/
#                          MASQUERADE all go through nft_compat).
#
# IMPORTANT: RK3576 kernel is statically built (empty /lib/modules), so every
# option MUST be =y (built-in). `=m` modules never load on this platform.
#
# TUN is only needed by rootless (slirp4netns); MACVLAN/IPVLAN only for
# macvlan/ipvlan network drivers — omitted to keep the change minimal.
CONFIG_POSIX_MQUEUE=y
CONFIG_BPF_SYSCALL=y
CONFIG_CGROUP_BPF=y
CONFIG_VETH=y
CONFIG_BRIDGE_NETFILTER=y
CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_MATCH_ADDRTYPE=y
CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_MATCH_CONNTRACK=y
CONFIG_OVERLAY_FS=y
CONFIG_MEMCG=y
CONFIG_NFT_COMPAT=y
CONFIG_NFT_NAT=y
